And Rob, how do you feel about that?
罗布，你对此有何感想？

00:35:48.512 --> 00:35:49.798
You helmed the episode.
你主持了这一集。

00:35:49.798 --> 00:35:52.369
He said is the best one to him at of 200 days.
他说这是他200天以来最好的一次。

00:35:52.369 --> 00:35:57.489
Well, I think it is what Josh said, it was like it's about the most mundane thing.
嗯，我想这就是乔什所说的，这就像是关于最平凡的事情。

00:35:57.489 --> 00:36:00.730
So the other, the emotion came through.
所以另一方面，情感也随之而来。

00:36:00.730 --> 00:36:03.409
If it was a more important event, it wouldn't have been as impactful.
如果这是一个更重要的事件，它的影响力就不会那么大。

00:36:03.409 --> 00:36:04.409
Right, right.
对，对。

00:36:04.409 --> 00:36:05.409
Yeah.
是的。

00:36:05.409 --> 00:36:14.929
You know, I just this morning started listening to Ezra Klein just posted like an episode of his show from 2023.
你知道，我今天早上才开始听 Ezra Klein 刚刚发布的内容，就像他 2023 年节目的一集一样。

00:36:14.929 --> 00:36:20.449
And it's with an author who wrote a book about hanging out.
和一位作家一起写了一本关于闲逛的书。

00:36:20.449 --> 00:36:27.849
the pleasures of hanging out with, for no particular reason, and how we don't do it anymore.
没有什么特别的原因，一起出去玩的乐趣，以及我们如何不再这样做了。

00:36:27.849 --> 00:36:49.809
And there's this real, you know, there's much written about the kind of loneliness epidemic and isolation and what the phones are doing to us, but they really interrogate, I've only listened to about 20 minutes of it, but they really interrogate this kind of lost world of hanging out, which this show is, if any show is a hanging out show, how we met mothers a hanging out show.
这是真实的，你知道，有很多关于孤独流行和孤立以及手机对我们做了什么的文章，但它们确实审问了，我只听了大约20分钟，但它们确实审问了这种失去的闲逛世界，这个节目就是，如果有节目是闲逛节目，我们如何遇见母亲就是闲逛节目。

00:36:49.809 --> 00:36:58.489
And they're just, they're sitting around that booth, drinking beer, making each other laugh, for no reason other than the delight in each other's company.
他们只是坐在那个摊位周围，喝着啤酒，互相开怀大笑，没有任何原因，只是为了彼此的陪伴而感到高兴。

00:36:58.489 --> 00:36:59.329
Yeah.
是的。

00:36:59.329 --> 00:37:13.769
And I think in the same way that Gen Z loves hearing about like 90s childhoods, like 80s, 90s analog childhoods, because it starts to feel like this lost world that they never got to know because they all had digital childhoods.
我认为，就像 Z 世代喜欢听到 90 年代的童年一样，就像 80 年代、90 年代的模拟童年一样，因为这开始让人感觉这是一个他们从未了解过的失落的世界，因为他们都有数字童年。

00:37:13.769 --> 00:37:28.869
And I think part of the, one of the things, Rob, that we're really interrogating on the show like the long tail of it, why people still watch it and care about it and other shows that were even more popular than we were on when we were on the air have kind of faded in the cultural memory.
我认为，罗布，我们真的在追问这个节目的一部分，就像它的长尾一样，为什么人们仍然观看它并关心它，而其他比我们播出时更受欢迎的节目已经在文化记忆中消失了。

00:37:28.869 --> 00:37:35.110
But for some reason, this show still has a beating heart and a place in the cultural firmament.
但出于某种原因，这部剧仍然有一颗跳动的心，并在文化领域占有一席之地。

00:37:35.110 --> 00:37:37.309
So, I think that's one of the reasons.
所以，我认为这是原因之一。

00:37:37.309 --> 00:37:48.030
There's something pleasurable about watching people face to face care about each other, live life together, share experiences together, celebrate holidays and super bowls together.
看着人们面对面互相关心、一起生活、一起分享经历、一起庆祝节日和超级碗是一件令人愉快的事情。

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:55.949
There's something, it touches something in us that we all want, but might not currently have.
有一些东西，它触动了我们内心的一些东西，我们都想要，但目前可能还没有。

00:37:55.949 --> 00:37:56.949
I think that's right.
我认为这是对的。

00:37:56.949 --> 00:37:57.949
I think it scratches an itch.
我认为它会让人发痒。

00:37:57.949 --> 00:38:02.389
I think there's something about seeing, even that they all decide this is important.
我认为观看是有一定意义的，即使他们都认为这很重要。

00:38:02.389 --> 00:38:05.829
They all decide it's so important not to hear this result.
他们都认为不听到这个结果非常重要。

00:38:05.829 --> 00:38:06.829
Yeah.
是的。

00:38:06.829 --> 00:38:17.309
And there's a funny thing, like even though I said they were a bit callous about poor Mark who died, there's a funny kind of thematic overlap of like, well, that's how it ends for everybody.
还有一件有趣的事情，就像尽管我说他们对死去的可怜的马克有点冷酷无情，但有一种有趣的主题重叠，就像，好吧，这就是每个人的结局。

00:38:17.309 --> 00:38:20.590
So eventually we all die at some point.
所以最终我们都会在某个时刻死去。

00:38:20.590 --> 00:38:25.389
And this idea of what's so great about the ending or the results, right?
这个关于结局或结果的伟大想法是什么，对吗？

00:38:25.389 --> 00:38:27.630
Who cares who won that game?
谁在乎谁赢了那场比赛？

00:38:27.630 --> 00:38:28.989
Who cares what the end is?
谁在乎结局是什么？

00:38:28.989 --> 00:38:32.630
The end is, you know, the ultimate end is we all die.
结局是，你知道，最终的结局是我们都死了。

00:38:34.269 --> 00:38:41.510
Well, that's also, but we better enjoy watching the fucking game together as the most important part.
好吧，那也是，但我们最好享受一起观看他妈的比赛，这是最重要的部分。

00:38:41.510 --> 00:38:43.909
The end result is not the most important thing.
最终的结果并不是最重要的。

00:38:43.909 --> 00:38:50.429
Well Craig, that's what our kind of response is when people say like, this could have been 15 minutes.
克雷格，这就是当人们说“这可能需要 15 分钟”时我们的反应。

00:38:50.429 --> 00:38:52.269
It's like, well, that's not an interesting show.
就好像，嗯，这不是一个有趣的节目。

00:38:52.269 --> 00:38:54.949
You know, like the entire show was always about the journey.
你知道，就像整个节目总是关于旅程一样。

00:38:54.949 --> 00:39:03.510
It was always about, and when people would get upset, you know, when the show was on the air, there were certain how I made your mother obsessives that were obsessed with who the mother was.
这总是关于，当人们感到不安时，你知道，当节目播出时，我如何让你的母亲着迷，他们着迷于母亲是谁。

00:39:03.510 --> 00:39:09.670
Like that was the big mystery to crack as if this was like a true crime podcast or something.
就像这是一个需要破解的大谜团，就好像这就像一个真正的犯罪播客或其他东西一样。

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:11.429
But that was never the thing.
但事实并非如此。

00:39:11.429 --> 00:39:13.630
I mean, it was a fun thing to hang it on.
我的意思是，把它挂起来是一件很有趣的事情。

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:18.170
and it probably helped you guys sell the show to have a kind of hook, but that was never the thing.
这可能有助于你们卖掉这部剧，因为它有一种吸引力，但事实并非如此。

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:23.489
The thing was always, how do we create a context where people can hang out and love each other and laugh?
问题始终是，我们如何创造一个人们可以闲逛、彼此相爱、欢笑的环境？

00:39:23.489 --> 00:39:26.769
And I think maybe Andrew was hinting at that in his thoughtful answer too.
我想也许安德鲁在他深思熟虑的回答中也暗示了这一点。

00:39:26.769 --> 00:39:31.849
Like there's something, this is in miniature the entire series in some way.
就像有什么东西一样，这在某种程度上是整个系列的缩影。

00:39:31.849 --> 00:39:34.250
Yeah, there's gonna be a result, there's gonna be a score.
是的，会有一个结果，会有一个分数。

00:39:34.250 --> 00:39:40.969
There will be a thing ascertained and the clock will stop and it'll be a result and they don't remember what the fucking result is.
将会有一件事被确定，时钟会停止，这将是一个结果，但他们不记得他妈的结果是什么。

00:39:40.969 --> 00:39:43.949
The whole episode is about that and you never hear what it is.
整集都是关于这个的，但你永远不会听到它是什么。

00:39:43.949 --> 00:39:47.829
And I think that's a really great way to say it's the journey that matters.
我认为这是一个很好的方式来表达旅程才是重要的。

00:39:47.829 --> 00:39:49.309
And it's being together that matters.
在一起才是最重要的。

00:39:49.309 --> 00:39:50.150
Yeah.
是的。

00:39:50.150 --> 00:39:59.630
Well, it wasn't also there was a little bit of a wink to the series, because in the narrate at the very end, he says like, even though you know how it's gonna end, that's not the point.
好吧，这个系列也没有一点眨眼的地方，因为在最后的叙述中，他说，即使你知道它会如何结束，但这不是重点。

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:00.630
That's not the point.
这不是重点。

00:40:00.630 --> 00:40:04.190
And he's telling his kids about their mother, they know how it is.
他正在向他的孩子们讲述他们的母亲，他们知道那是怎么回事。

00:40:04.190 --> 00:40:06.190
So it's not about that.
所以不是这个问题。

00:40:06.190 --> 00:40:07.469
